  "summary": "# Daily Health-Tech Digest — October 22, 2026\n\nRegulatory momentum continues around artificial intelligence and drug approvals, with the FDA and industry moving to establish clearer oversight frameworks. The AMA is pushing for risk-based GenAI regulation to ensure accountability as these tools take on clinical functions, while an FDA official suggested AI systems may need evaluation similar to physician competency. Meanwhile, GSK secured two significant wins: FDA approval for Hibsago, a novel hepatitis B drug using antisense oligonucleotide technology to target viral replication, and accelerated approval for Jemperli in advanced rectal cancer with specific genetic markers.\n\nHealthcare system economics and interoperability remain focal concerns. Epic is expanding system connectivity while launching a prescription price comparison tool for 2027, and Oracle-AstraZeneca are collaborating on smarter clinical trials. Several observers highlight structural challenges beyond technology: the gap between diagnosis and operational execution in AI implementation, sustainability issues with cross-subsidization in health systems, and delivery failures for existing antibiotics due to market rather than innovation constraints.\n\nThe investment landscape shows continued biotech momentum, with multiple funding rounds and IPOs spanning rare disease drugs, RNAi therapeutics, AI-driven molecular discovery, and behavioral health platforms. Notably, startups are targeting specific gaps like ostomy device alternatives, rare endocrine disorders, and clinician staffing optimization. CMS enforcement activity expanded into adult day care fraud investigation in New York, while Medicare innovation continues with Judi Health's new employer-sponsored Part D plan.",
